1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A Bernoulli trial has two outcomes: success with probability p, failure with q = 1 - p. At p = 0.3 the two bars are 0.70 and 0.30.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Ten independent trials, each with the same p. Here three of the ten came out a success.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
X is the count: X = X1 + X2 + ... + X10, where each Xi is 1 for a success and 0 otherwise.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Three conditions: n fixed in advance, p the same every trial, trials independent. Then E[X] = np = 3 and Var = npq = 2.1.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
P(X = k) = C(n,k) p^k q^(n-k), for k from 0 to n. This is a mass function: the probability sits on the integers.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
At n = 10 and p = 0.3 the tallest bar is k = 3: C(10,3) = 120 sequences, each of probability 0.3^3 x 0.7^7 = 0.002224.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
120 x 0.002224 = 0.266828. The two end bars are 0.7^10 = 0.028248 and 0.3^10 = 0.000006.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The eleven bars total 1.000000000000, and nothing outside k = 0 to 10 carries any mass.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Neighbouring masses differ by one multiplication: P(k+1)/P(k) = ((n-k)/(k+1)) x (p/q). No factorial is ever needed.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
The ratios measured from the bars are 4.285714, 1.928571, 1.142857, then 0.75 — and the formula gives the same ten numbers.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The peak is at floor((n+1)p). At n = 10, p = 0.3 that is floor(3.3) = 3; at n = 9, p = 0.5 it is 5, and 4 ties with it at 0.246094.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Not np: at n = 5 and p = 0.3 the mean is 1.5, but the peak is at 1, because 0.360150 beats 0.308700.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Each trial contributes p to the mean, so E[X] = np — by linearity, with no independence needed.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Each trial has variance p - p^2 = pq, and independent variances add, so Var(X) = npq.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Summing the bars agrees at every parameter pair: at n = 100, p = 0.3 the mean is 30 and the variance 21, by both routes.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
With the mean held at 6, the variance is 6(1-p): 5.4 at p = 0.1, 3.0 at p = 0.5 and 0.6 at p = 0.9. Rare events give the quietest count.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Two random 8-bit strings. In each position the two bits differ with probability 1/2, independently of the other positions.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
So the Hamming distance D is the number of successes in n fair trials: D has a binomial distribution with parameters n and 1/2.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
P(D = d) = C(n,d)/2^n. At n = 8 and d = 3 that is 56/256 = 0.218750, the highlighted bar.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Counting agrees: 2^8 x C(8,3) = 14336 pairs out of 65536. The distractor C(n,d)/2^d forgets the positions that agree.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A set of size 2n, one fair coin per element, an element chosen on a head: the count chosen is Binomial(2n, 1/2).

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
P(X = n) = C(2n,n) (1/2)^n (1/2)^n = C(2n,n)/4^n, because two to the 2n is four to the n.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The other options fail: C(2n,n)/2^n drops a factor, 1/C(2n,n) inverts the count, and 1/2 is only the limit.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
It is a shrinking probability: 0.5 at n = 1, 0.375 at n = 2, and 0.112275 at n = 25 against the limit 0.112838.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Two independent binomials with the same p add by adding trial counts: Bin(4,0.3) + Bin(6,0.3) is Bin(10,0.3).

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Convolving the two mass functions reproduces the eleven bars of Binomial(10, 0.3) with largest difference 0.000000000000.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Different p and it breaks: Bin(2,0.2) + Bin(3,0.8) is not Binomial(5, 0.5) — P(1) is 0.064000 against 0.156250.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The mean 2.8 and the variance 0.8 add up either way — so equal moments do not make the sum binomial.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Hold the mean at np = 3 and let n grow. The mass function slides towards Poisson(3), drawn here as dots over the bars.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
At n = 10, p = 0.3 the largest gap is 0.04279; P(0) is 0.028248 against the Poisson 0.049787.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Each factor of ten in n buys a factor of ten in accuracy: 3.432e-3 at n = 100, 3.368e-4 at 1000, down to 3.361e-6 at 100000.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The other limit is the normal, when np and nq are both large. Same family, two different directions.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Trials until the first success: P(N = n) = q^(n-1) p. This is the geometric distribution, and N has no upper bound.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Its mean is 1/p = 3.3333 and its median is 2. The chance of at least one success in ten trials is 1 - 0.7^10 = 0.971752.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
P(the 3rd success lands on trial 5) = C(4,2) x 0.3^3 x 0.7^2 = 0.079380 — not C(5,3) x 0.3^3 x 0.7^2 = 0.132300.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The negative binomial counts trials until k successes, with mean k/p = 10 here. The geometric is the case k = 1.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Draw four from ten with three marked, without replacement: p changes on every draw, so this is hypergeometric, not binomial.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
P(1) is 0.500000 against the binomial 0.411600, and the variance shrinks by (N-n)/(N-1) = 0.666667 to 0.560000.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Break any one condition and the arithmetic changes: no fixed n, a p that drifts, or trials that share a cause.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The procedure: check the three conditions, decide which quantity is fixed, then use np, npq, floor((n+1)p) and the ratio.

